From c5679712c756e0f960a92615d3cbc5024fe06d6f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thomas Steiner <tomac@chromium.org>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 11:44:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 6/8] [iOS] Manage Gemma engines across sessions, memory, and
 the background

Sessions keep a conversation with their engine, which holds the engine
and its KV cache. Evicting an engine now has its sessions let go of
those conversations, so the engine really goes, and sessions survive it:
their next turn reloads the engine and rebuilds the conversation from
the history the page sends with every turn. An epoch per engine keeps
conversations of evicted engines from being reused.

Gemma runs one turn at a time browser-wide, so an engine being replaced
is idle and freed before the next one loads. A second engine stays
loaded only while the app has room for it, so two pages with different
needs do not reload on every turn. Engines are evicted on memory
warnings, also while sessions exist, a minute after the last session is
gone, and 20 seconds after the browser goes to the background, within
background task time, since iOS suspends the app soon after.

iOS lets GPU work in the background neither run nor fail, so it would
hang. GPU turns wait for the foreground, an engine that finished loading
in the background does not start generating, and a GPU turn running as
the browser goes there is stopped and runs again in the foreground
unless part of its answer reached the page already. Sessions are now
counted synchronously, and loads, evictions, and the memory after them
are recorded with the stats.

On an iPhone 11 Pro Max, two pages using the text and the multimodal
file at the same time peak at 1.2 GB of footprint, against 2.2 GB
before, and a backgrounded browser drops to 0.1 GB.
